The problem: In the journey, step 2 expects pressing Tab while suggestions are visible (#awesomplete_list_1) to move focus to #dietary. However currently after typing "Ber", suggestions appear, focus goes to first suggestion Bern (<li ...>) because tabbing within combobox may go into list items due to tabindex="0"? Actually the issue: When suggestions are open, the combobox input likely still has focus? But observed shows after pressing Tab, focus moved to the li element (first suggestion) instead of moving to #dietary. So the expected behavior: Tab should exit the combobox and go to next field, not navigate within listbox.

In Awesomplete, they handle keydown: they intercept Tab only if me.selected && me.tabSelect (which defaults false). They have:

```
else if (c === 9 && me.selected && me.tabSelect) {
    evt.preventDefault();
    me.select(undefined, undefined, evt);
}
```

Thus if tabSelect is false (default), Tab is not prevented, so default behavior occurs: focus moves to next focusable element after the input? However the input might still be focused? Actually after typing, the suggestions list is visible and each li has tabindex="0". So the order of tabbable elements: input#city, then li#awesomplete_list_1_item_0 (Bern), then li item 1 (Berlin), etc., then maybe #dietary. Since the input may lose focus when dropdown opens? Usually focus stays on input, but the list items are not in tab order unless they have tabindex. They added tabindex='0' to each li for Talkback screen reader. That makes them tabbable. So pressing Tab moves focus to first li.

Goal: While suggestions are shown, one Tab press moves focus to the next form field (#dietary). So we need to make Tab skip the list items and go to next field. Options: remove tabindex from li items when list is open, or make them not focusable (tabindex="-1") or handle keydown to prevent default and move focus to next field.

Simplest: In the keydown handler, when Tab is pressed and the list is opened, we should prevent default and move focus to the next focusable element after the combobox (i.e., #dietary). Or we could set tabSelect to true and make selecting on Tab close the list and put value in input, then focus moves to next field after closing? Actually if tabSelect true, they prevent default and call select, which closes list and puts value in input, then focus remains on input? After select, they close list, but focus stays on input (since they didn't move it). Then another Tab would be needed to go to next field. Not desired.

Better: Adjust the li elements to not be focusable (remove tabindex) when list is open, or set tabindex="-1". However they added tabindex='0' for Talkback screen reader; maybe we can keep it but adjust keydown to ignore Tab when list open and move focus to next field.

Let's look at the keydown handler in awesomplete.js:

```
"keydown": function(evt) {
    var c = evt.keyCode;

    // If the dropdown `ul` is in view, then act on keydown for the following keys:
    // Enter / Esc / Up / Down
    if(me.opened) {
        if (c === 13 && me.selected) { // Enter
            evt.preventDefault();
            me.select(undefined, undefined, evt);
        }
        else if (c === 9 && me.selected && me.tabSelect) {
            evt.preventDefault();
            me.select(undefined, undefined, evt);
        }
        else if (c === 27) { // Esc
            me.close({ reason: "esc" });
        }
        else if (c === 38 || c === 40) { // Down/Up arrow
            evt.preventDefault();
            me[c === 38? "previous" : "next"]();
        }
    }
}
```

So currently Tab does nothing special unless tabSelect true and selected. It falls through, allowing default behavior (which will move focus to next focusable element, which is the first li because it has tabindex). To fix, we can add a case for Tab when opened: prevent default and move focus to the next focusable element after the combobox (i.e., shift focus to the next element in tab order after the input). We can try to focus the next element using something like:

```
if (c === 9 && me.opened) {
    evt.preventDefault();
    // move focus to the next focusable element after the input
    var input = me.input;
    // find next focusable element
    var walker = document.createTreeWalker(
        document.body,
        NodeFilter.SHOW_ELEMENT,
        function(node) {
            return node.tabIndex >= 0 && !node.disabled && (
                node.tagName === 'INPUT' ||
                node.tagName === 'SELECT' ||
                node.tagName === 'TEXTAREA' ||
                node.tagName === 'BUTTON' ||
                node.tagName === 'A' && node.href
            );
        },
        false
    );
    walker.currentNode = input;
    var next = walker.nextNode();
    if (next) {
        next.focus();
    } else {
        // fallback: blur?
    }
}
```

But that's complex. Simpler: we can just let the default behavior happen but ensure that the li items are not in tab order. Remove tabindex attribute from li items when created, or set tabindex="-1". However they added tabindex='0' for Talkback screen reader; removing it may affect accessibility for Talkback but maybe acceptable? The requirement: we need to preserve functional behavior; making list items not focusable may break keyboard navigation via arrow keys? Arrow keys still work because they rely on keydown handling, not tabindex. Screen readers may still read options via aria-activedescendant. So removing tabindex should be fine.

Thus edit awesomplete.js where li items are created: In the ITEM function, they set `"tabindex": '0'`. Change that to `-1` or remove. Let's see the ITEM function:

```
_.ITEM = function (text, input, item_id) {
    var html = input.trim() === "" ? text : text.replace(RegExp($.regExpEscape(input.trim()), "gi"), "<mark>$&</mark>");
    return $.create("li", {
        innerHTML: html,
        "role": "option",
        "aria-selected": "false",
        "tabindex": '0', // for the Talkback screen reader
        "id": "awesomplete_list_" + this.count + "_item_" + item_id
    });
};
```

Change tabindex to '-1'.

Alternatively, we could conditionally set tabindex based on a flag, but simplest: change to '-1'.

